The '''Tragic Week''' ({{lang-bl|La Semana Trájika}}), alternatively the '''Seven Days of Lead''' ({{lang-bl|Sete Dias de Plomo}}) was a series of violent reprisals and counter-reprisals during the [[Baliscan Revolution]] between the Baliscan military and anarchists, freemasons, socialists and revolutionaries of Castejón and other major cities throughout Balisca in the autumn of 1919. 

It began on 21 April following the [[storming of the Baliscan Cabildo]] by rebelling soldiers and civilians in reaction to increasingly violent repression by the state. The successful coup against the [[Confederal Congress]] and the seizure the Cabildo [[W:Power of the deed|instigated]] the second, more violent phase of the Baliscan Revolution, ultimately leading to a significant rise in {{W|political violence}} nationwide. For uninvolved citizens of Balisca, the seven days of violence, displacement and uncertainty were horrific. While most fighting occurred between opposing armed factions, assaulting or defending the growing power of the workers' councils, artillery and rifle fire inflicted substantial losses among uninvolved civilians and major damage to property in the capital's downtown. 

